// The way out of the middle of a program.
//
//     if n < 0 {
//         panic("a length cannot be negative")
//     }
//
// Section 8 named this as the gap: "a fn deep in a program that has found it
// cannot go on returns `never` and has nothing to call", and said that a
// `%symbol` declaration of somebody else's `exit` is not an answer for it.
// This is the answer -- the runtime's own, which says why before it goes.
//
// **It returns `never`, and that is what makes it useful.** `never` is the
// empty type, so an expression of it agrees with anything beside it: a call to
// this stands where a value of any type was wanted, and nothing after it is
// reached. That is what lets it be the other arm of a match, or the end of a
// fn that has no answer to give.
//
// **It stops the process rather than unwinding.** Nothing here unwinds -- no
// exception, no `catch`, and no way for a callee to return on its caller's
// behalf -- so a program that panics ends, with the status 101 and the message
// on the error stream. `runtime/src/stop.rs` is the other half of this file.
//
// It is *not* what a failing test uses. `test.ft` counts an assertion that did
// not hold and lets the test go on, because stopping there would abandon every
// test after it; the reason is argued out in that file. This is for a program
// that has found it cannot go on, which is the other thing.

%symbol("__rt_panic")
pub fn panic(why: str): never;
